Hi @nishantrevur, sorry for the slow reply — and thanks for opening #60339, it's good momentum for this area. To answer your question: yes, I'm still driving this PR. Recent work since your comment: coordinated suffix GPU rebuilds across TP ranks (33e3b704f1), suffix-gpu 0.1.3 with the graph-capture fixes, and most recently moving the drafter's CUDA graph onto the standard CUDAGraphWrapper/BatchDescriptor lifecycle (773a009548 — power-of-two buckets, shared decoder graph pool, follows the target model's graph mode) plus a dummy-run guard (2f7a6c8080).

On the MRV2 port specifically — it's further along than it may look from this PR's description. The working port lives on zip95297/suffix-gpu-mrv2 in my fork (branch zip95297/suffix-gpu-mrv2): GSM8K parity with V1 (0.756 V1 / 0.762 V2), acceptance-rate e2e > 0.8 on both runners, and the whole propose path captured as a single CUDA graph (kernels run over the full slot space with an active mask, so shapes are step-invariant). The honest caveat is throughput: on Spec-Bench, V2 was 17–28% behind V1 at k=5 and 43–56% at k=16 while async_nospec stayed within ±1.6%. Root cause is exactly what your adaptive-verification column attacks: V2 had no worker-side pre-forward trim, so every decode request paid the full (k+1)-slot forward (plus the drafter scanning the full slot space, ~6–7 ms/step at low concurrency). My plan was to wait for #40704 and reuse its device-side trimming — now that it has merged, that path is open and I'll rebase onto it.

Given all that, I'd suggest we avoid landing two parallel GPU suffix implementations under two method names. I agree with @njhill that on V2 this should just be suffix (V1 keeps Arctic). Concretely, I see the split as: your hash-index corpus is a genuinely nice answer to the suffix array's memory cost and TP rebuild complexity, while the CUDA-graph'd drafter path, warmup and metrics accounting in this PR (and the mrv2 branch above) are the pieces your draft currently doesn't have — the propose path there launches kernels eagerly every step, which is precisely the fixed overhead that hurts at low/mid concurrency. Happy to pair on combining them;

